cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) models economic and organizational activity through
  a real-resource or capability state K, actor-specific exchange-value and capital
  representations K_i, structured subjective states P_i, and actor-side action or
  process records A_i. The field is not an independent scalar value; it is a derived
  action-generating configuration of resources, capabilities, subjective states,
  capital representations, and projection-specified relations and constraints.
  Action generation is separated into feasible, cognitively or behaviorally
  available, and admissible action sets. Forecasts are represented as
  projection-selected outcome bundles, while current belief and expectation
  components of P_i support forecast generation and current preference and valuation
  components support evaluation. Multi-actor realized events are linked by shared
  event identity and constructed by reconciling participant-side records rather than
  discarding them as duplicates. Resource endpoint change is represented through a
  projection-defined difference operator rather than universal subtraction. Time is
  typed explicitly: calendar time is a model index, available time or remaining
  workable capacity may be represented in K, and actual labor time or hours worked are
  interval activity records in A or a labor measure. VFT distinguishes resource
  capability, realized use experience, and exchange-value representation. Its
  realized-use outcome is interval-indexed and may be called a VFT-specific use-value
  quantity when connecting to economic categories; general additivity and an
  instantaneous rate are not assumed. A rate may be introduced only in projections
  that separately define an appropriate cumulative realized-use process. Formal
  accounting is treated as a projection rather than a universal causal layer.
  Exchange-value residuals may receive projection-specific interpretations such as
  surplus, profit, income, or valuation gain. Organizations or companies can be
  modeled as actors, while a business field is a local action-generating configuration
  that supports repeatable actor-resource transformation, exchange, service, or
  beneficiary relations; a business is the activity system centered on such a field.
  Entrepreneurship and new business development can be represented through
  business-oriented field formation. VFT retains resource-realization, activity-flow,
  and P-downside as required constitutive rationality axioms. Their evaluation,
  judgment, and execution may be distributed across multiple actors, roles,
  organizational layers, or institutions, while each decision projection must define
  ex ante admissibility, exclusion, or comparison rules for all three axioms. Marxian
  categories such as use-value, labor, exchange-value, surplus, and accumulation can
  be represented through projection-specific specializations.
